To use different filters in Spring Security based on the value of the `Referer` header, you have two main approaches:

### 1. Multiple SecurityFilterChain Beans with RequestMatcher

Since Spring Security 5.0, you can define multiple `SecurityFilterChain` beans, each with its own `RequestMatcher`. For header-based matching, you can use `RequestHeaderRequestMatcher` or implement a custom `RequestMatcher` for more complex logic.

**Example:**

```java
@Bean
public SecurityFilterChain refererAFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http
        .securityMatcher(new RequestHeaderRequestMatcher("Referer", "https://refererA.com"))
        .addFilterBefore(new FilterForRefererA(), UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class)
        .authorizeHttpRequests(authz -> authz.anyRequest().authenticated());
    return http.build();
}

@Bean
public SecurityFilterChain refererBFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http
        .securityMatcher(new RequestHeaderRequestMatcher("Referer", "https://refererB.com"))
        .addFilterBefore(new FilterForRefererB(), UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class)
        .authorizeHttpRequests(authz -> authz.anyRequest().authenticated());
    return http.build();
}

@Bean
public SecurityFilterChain defaultFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http
        .authorizeHttpRequests(authz -> authz.anyRequest().authenticated());
    return http.build();
}
```

If you need more complex matching (e.g., partial matches or regex), implement a custom `RequestMatcher`.

### 2. Single FilterChain with a Custom Filter

Alternatively, you can define a single filter chain and add a custom filter that inspects the `Referer` header and delegates to different logic or filters accordingly.

**Example:**

```java
public class RefererDelegatingFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {
    private final Filter filterA;
    private final Filter filterB;

    public RefererDelegatingFilter(Filter filterA, Filter filterB) {
        this.filterA = filterA;
        this.filterB = filterB;
    }

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response, FilterChain chain)
            throws ServletException, IOException {
        String referer = request.getHeader("Referer");
        if ("https://refererA.com".equals(referer)) {
            filterA.doFilter(request, response, chain);
        } else if ("https://refererB.com".equals(referer)) {
            filterB.doFilter(request, response, chain);
        } else {
            chain.doFilter(request, response);
        }
    }
}
```

Register this filter in your security configuration with `.addFilterBefore(...)`.

### Notes

- The `Referer` header can be spoofed by clients, so do not rely on it for critical security decisions.
- Multiple filter chains are cleaner and more modular, but a custom filter may be simpler for some use cases.
- If you need help with a specific filter implementation or matching logic, please provide more details.

