**My Answer:**

To implement a guided process framework for user registration in ASP.NET Core with a single-page UI, you can use a combination of ASP.NET Core MVC (or Razor Pages), HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. The idea is to present all registration fields on one page, but only one field is enabled and highlighted at a time. The user is guided step-by-step, with contextual help shown for each field, and cannot interact with other fields until the current one is completed.

**Step-by-Step Implementation:**

1. **Backend Setup (ASP.NET Core):**
   - Create a UserRegistration model with properties like Name, Email, Password, etc.
   - Create a controller with actions to render the registration page and handle form submission.

2. **Frontend (Single Page with Guided Steps):**
   - Render all input fields in the form, but only the first field is enabled initially.
   - Use JavaScript to manage which field is active, highlight it, and show help text.
   - When the user completes a field and clicks "Next" (or presses Enter), validate the input, then enable the next field and disable the previous one.
   - On the last step, show a "Submit" button to send all data to the server.

**Example:**

*UserRegistration.cs (Model):*
```csharp
public class UserRegistration
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public string Email { get; set; }
    public string Password { get; set; }
}
```

*RegistrationController.cs:*
```csharp
public class RegistrationController : Controller
{
    [HttpGet]
    public IActionResult Index() => View();

    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Index(UserRegistration model)
    {
        if (ModelState.IsValid)
        {
            // Save user, etc.
            return RedirectToAction("Success");
        }
        return View(model);
    }

    public IActionResult Success() => View();
}
```

*Index.cshtml (View):*
```html
@model UserRegistration

<style>
    .highlight { border: 2px solid #007bff; }
    .help-text { color: #555; margin-bottom: 10px; }
    input[disabled] { background: #eee; }
</style>

<form id="regForm" asp-action="Index" method="post">
    <div>
        <label>Name</label>
        <input id="name" name="Name" type="text" />
        <div class="help-text" id="help-name">Enter your full name.</div>
    </div>
    <div>
        <label>Email</label>
        <input id="email" name="Email" type="email" disabled />
        <div class="help-text" id="help-email">Enter a valid email address.</div>
    </div>
    <div>
        <label>Password</label>
        <input id="password" name="Password" type="password" disabled />
        <div class="help-text" id="help-password">Choose a strong password.</div>
    </div>
    <button type="button" id="nextBtn">Next</button>
    <button type="submit" id="submitBtn" style="display:none;">Submit</button>
</form>

<script>
    const steps = [
        { id: 'name', help: 'help-name' },
        { id: 'email', help: 'help-email' },
        { id: 'password', help: 'help-password' }
    ];
    let current = 0;

    function setStep(idx) {
        steps.forEach((step, i) => {
            const input = document.getElementById(step.id);
            input.disabled = i !== idx;
            input.classList.toggle('highlight', i === idx);
        });
        document.getElementById(steps[idx].id).focus();
        document.getElementById('nextBtn').style.display = idx < steps.length - 1 ? '' : 'none';
        document.getElementById('submitBtn').style.display = idx === steps.length - 1 ? '' : 'none';
    }

    document.getElementById('nextBtn').onclick = function() {
        const input = document.getElementById(steps[current].id);
        if (!input.value) {
            alert('Please fill this field.');
            return;
        }
        if (current < steps.length - 1) {
            current++;
            setStep(current);
        }
    };

    setStep(0);
</script>
```

**How it works:**
- Only the current field is enabled and highlighted.
- Help text is shown for the current field.
- User clicks "Next" to proceed; only after filling the current field can they move on.
- On the last step, "Submit" is shown.

